WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.423 --> 00:00:07.590
CHRIS LATIMER: Hi, and
welcome to our session today.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.840
My name is Chris Latimer.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.060
I'm one of the product
managers here at Google Cloud.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.450
And today I'm
going to be talking

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.290
about building and managing
APIs for serverless

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.125
with Google Cloud.

00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:18.500
There are going
to be five things

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.830
we're going to look at today.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.288
We're going to
start off by looking

00:00:21.288 --> 00:00:22.880
at how we can build services.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.150
We're going to use the Cloud
Code plug in that GCP provides

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.710
the developers to
make it easy to get

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.333
started with GCP development.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:32.000
We're going to look
at how we can deploy

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.542
those services to Cloud Run.

00:00:33.542 --> 00:00:35.000
And then we're
going to look at how

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.190
we can combine multiple
services together

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.990
to form an API using GCP's
brand-new API gateway, which

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.970
is just announced in beta.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.470
Once we have that
API created, we're

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.600
going to see how we
can secure that API.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.125
And then finally, once
everything's up and running,

00:00:51.125 --> 00:00:52.500
we're going to
take a look at how

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.530
we can monitor that API
using Cloud Logging and Cloud

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.870
Monitoring so that, if
anything's not working the way

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:02.665
we expect, we can get
alerted to that fact.

00:01:02.665 --> 00:01:04.290
For our first step,
we're going to look

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.030
at how we can build services.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.460
We're going to use the
Cloud Code plugin that

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.260
comes with IntelliJ.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.090
Now, you could also
use visual code

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.047
if that's your preferred IDE.

00:01:16.047 --> 00:01:17.880
Now, there are a few
steps that I've already

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.840
taken ahead of time.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.400
So I've already
installed IntelliJ.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.340
I've already installed
the Cloud Code plugin.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.562
I also have installed
Docker and the gcloud CLI.

00:01:26.562 --> 00:01:28.020
So if you're going
to follow along,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.650
you may want to do the same
steps on your workstation.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.450
So let's jump to the
demo and take a look

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.540
at how we can build a service
using the Cloud Code plugin

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.300
and then get that
deployed to Cloud Run.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.800
To get started in
IntelliJ, we're

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.960
going to select
create a new project.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.530
And we are going to select the
Cloud Run option that comes

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.120
with the Cloud Code plugin.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.360
And I'm going to pick
the Java version.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.420
We'll give this a name.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.410
We'll call it customers
API and click Finish.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.640
Once this is done,
IntelliJ is going

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.070
to look at the
project structure.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:05.910
It's going to detect that
we have a Maven POM file.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.090
We can add that POM file and
switch this over to a Maven

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.758
project now inside of IntelliJ.

00:02:10.758 --> 00:02:13.050
That's going to give us all
the standard Maven features

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.770
that we'd want.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.320
If we take a look
over at the code base,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.040
we'll see that, within
the file system,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.570
the Cloud Code plugin has
given us a good starting point.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.570
It gives us a
Spring Boot app that

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.680
will run right out of the box.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.070
So if I configure my
SDK and tell IntelliJ

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:35.650
I want to use Java
11, I can then

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.410
click over here on
this spring-boot

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.840
and select the
spring-boot run option.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.912
This is going to start
my application locally.

00:02:43.912 --> 00:02:46.120
And so after a few seconds,
this is going to come up.

00:02:49.298 --> 00:02:50.840
And it's going to
start successfully.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.750
And we can verify
that by looking over

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.990
here at the log messages that
are printed to the console.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.770
We see that it successfully
started, now listening

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:02.730
and accepting traffic.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.540
If I come into my browser
now and I hit localhost 8080,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.720
we can see that this
app is in fact running.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.805
So right out of the box, we can
get a web application working.

00:03:16.805 --> 00:03:19.180
But what we really want is we
want a microservice that we

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.370
can deploy to Cloud Run.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.890
And so rather than walking
through the individual steps

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.430
of how to create an API inside
of Spring Boot which is well

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.360
documented elsewhere
on the internet,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.350
I have already pre-configured
this in my git repository.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.540
So I'm going to start
off by checking out

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.140
a tag here called basic API.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.173
And then once I do this, we
can go back into the IDE.

00:03:42.173 --> 00:03:43.840
And here, if we look
at the file system,

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.070
we're going to see now that,
instead of the web app,

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.162
we have a controller, an
entity, and a service.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:50.620
Inside the controller--
you can see

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.630
this is a REST controller-- it
has a single mapping to slash.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.870
And the way that we are going to
process these incoming requests

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.690
is we're going to use a service
called customer service.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.850
Looking inside here, this
is just a mock service.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.650
I don't have a
database behind here.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.290
But it is going to populate
a list of entities,

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.300
a list of entities
called customer,

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.120
that has a number of different
fields associated with it.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.550
We can now run this the
same way that we did before.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.079
So we'll use the Spring Boot
plugin, use the run target.

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:22.540
Again, we're going to see
messages printed out here

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:23.410
to the console.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.900
And after a few
seconds, we're going

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.250
to see one that says that our
app is started successfully.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.590
And if we look, scroll
over here to the right,

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.650
we do see that same message.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:38.960
So once that's started,
we can come now

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.250
over into the terminal window.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:45.740
And let's issue a cURL
command on localhost 8080.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:50.770
We can pretty print this JSON
with the Python JSON tool.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:51.880
And there we go.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.910
Our API is successfully
now running locally

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.020
here on my workstation.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.770
Now we're ready to deploy our
microservice into Cloud Run.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.540
So let's click on Edit
Configurations up here.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.740
This is going to bring up a
form that comes with Cloud Code.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.480
We can pick our GCP project.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.370
We can pick our
region that we want

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.800
to deploy our service into.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.630
We can select a
service name and then

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.850
whether we want to have
authentication or not.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:16.870
In this case, we do.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.310
So we'll select that.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:20.680
Last thing we need to
specify is where we should

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.220
upload this container image.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.450
In this case, we're going to use
the Google Container Registry.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.630
So we'll give it a name
here of customers API

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:32.380
and note that my project
is also specified here.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:36.790
Also important to note that I've
already pre-enabled the Google

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:40.840
Container Registry on my project
before I started this demo.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.330
So you may get an error
if you try to upload this

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:47.160
and you haven't enabled that
service on your project.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.460
Now let's go ahead and save
our configuration here.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.990
And now let's run it.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.660
So as this runs,
it's essentially

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.070
going to do all
the steps that we

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:56.750
need to actually
get our container up

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.730
and running on Cloud Run.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:00.770
So it's going to build
a container locally.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:02.480
It's going to interact with GCR.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.000
It's going to create the
necessary tags that it needs.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.630
If it finds the
container in the cache,

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.380
it's not going to rebuild it.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.790
And then it's ultimately going
to deploy this to Cloud Run.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.610
And after a few seconds
here, what we should see

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.680
is that it'll come back
and say it's deployed

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.470
and now accepting traffic.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.930
And traffic is going to
be accessible via this URL

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.800
that you see right here.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.650
Now, remember we
secured this API.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.170
And so if we come back
to the terminal window

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.918
and we try to cURL
that URL, we're

00:06:32.918 --> 00:06:35.210
going to get a message saying
that your client does not

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:38.420
have permissions, essentially,
to make this call.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.120
And that was back here
on this configuration

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.370
where we said require
authentication.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:46.310
If we had said allow
unauthenticated connections,

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:47.930
then we would have
been able to access

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:49.760
this API just fine as is.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.250
But we really want the
gateway to be that entry point

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:53.090
into our API.

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:54.440
So we want to say--

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.285
we want to tell Cloud Run to
authenticate these requests so

00:06:57.285 --> 00:06:59.660
that all of our clients have
to come through the gateway.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.260
And the only
authenticated client

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.560
that's going to be able
to call into our Cloud Run

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.880
back indirectly is
going to be the gateway.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:08.950
So we see that the
API has been deployed.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.810
We can hit it here
locally using cURL.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.440
But let's jump over
to Cloud Console

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.880
and just verify that
everything looks fine there.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.910
We'll navigate
down to Cloud Run.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.720
And just as expected, we see
the customer API deployed,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.170
green checkbox next to it.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:26.320
Everything is looking good here.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.170
So let's jump back into the
presentation and recap what

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.150
we've seen so far.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.740
All right, so coming
back from the demo,

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.040
let's take a look
at what we just saw.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:37.960
The first thing we saw
is how to get started

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.580
using the Cloud Code plugin.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.500
This set up a nice
workspace for us.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.470
It set our file system up so
that we had all of the pieces

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.410
that we needed to start
building our actual service

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.220
that we're interested in.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.950
We took that initial
starting point.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.690
And we used it to
create a nice RESTful

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.700
service using Spring Boot.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.400
And then once we were satisfied
that that was running locally

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.220
the way that we wanted,
we configured the IDE

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.310
to deploy that into Cloud Run.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.380
Once that was there, we
saw that the deployment

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:06.260
worked successfully.

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.890
And now we have our service
up and running exactly

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.260
as we hoped it would.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:15.250
And so currently, this is what
our cloud instance looks like.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:20.190
We have a single customer API
that's running in Cloud Run.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:24.270
And if we wanted to, we could
have a client make calls

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.610
directly to this service.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.990
If all we had was a single
service and a single client,

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:30.060
this is not such a bad option.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.560
We could create a
service account.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.720
We could give that service
account to the client.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:34.740
They could make calls.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:38.333
And everything would
work pretty good.

00:08:38.333 --> 00:08:39.750
The problem that's
going to happen

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.330
is that, as we start adding
more and more services,

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.280
we're putting more of
a burden on our client.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:45.655
They're going to
have to remember

00:08:45.655 --> 00:08:48.480
which service is responsible for
which piece of functionality.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.630
They're going to have to route
to the right place each time.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.430
And that can be inconvenient.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:57.300
It can also be inconvenient
for us as the API providers,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.910
because now we have to think
about who we're going to break

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.860
whenever we make a change.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.870
What would be better is if
we could put a single API

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.460
facade in front
of these services

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.460
so that, for our
clients, we can give them

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.740
one consistent interface
that they can use.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.170
And we can insulate
ourselves from those clients.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.950
We have flexibility to scale,
and deploy, and make changes

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.610
to our backend services without
worrying that our clients are

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.600
going to need to change.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.380
So let's jump into our
next demo and take a look

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.940
at how we can use services that
are deployed onto Cloud Run,

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.440
put an API gateway
in front of those

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.450
to create one consistent
API facade for our clients.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.270
So let's jump back
into the cloud console

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.730
here and navigate to Cloud Run.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.230
Like we showed on
the presentation,

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.810
we want to be able to
have an API gateway that's

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.660
going to sit in front
of multiple services

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.820
so that our API
clients don't have

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:53.060
to worry about which service
to route which requests to.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.490
We just want them to
have one, nice API facade

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:56.890
that they can call into.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.720
And we want API gateway to
handle both the security

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:01.970
on that API as
well as the routing

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:04.190
so that we send those
requests to the right service

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.810
on the backend.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.640
To configure API
gateway, we need

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.610
to have an open API
specification file.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.850
And it needs to have all of
the resources in our API.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:15.350
So in this case,
you can see that we

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:19.010
have a customers resource,
an accounts resource,

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.110
and an orders resource.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:24.320
Once we have that setup,
we need to configure

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.090
the value for host.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.850
Now, this may be a
little bit surprising

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:32.090
if you're used to
using open API.

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:34.490
The value that we're going
to put here in the host field

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:38.540
is going to be a combination
of the identifier

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:41.750
that we want to use for the
API here in this field called

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:44.640
API ID as well as our project.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.560
And this is going to conform
to the standard service naming

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:51.720
structure that's
used within Google.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.880
So for right now, just
think about this as,

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.880
this is just the format
that you have to follow.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.680
Don't think too much about why
this format is the way it is.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:02.720
This is just the way that
API gateway gets configured.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.400
And then here on
the title field,

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.190
we want to put in
that same API ID.

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:10.210
And we'll see in later
steps that we're going to--

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:11.990
that this value that
we put in for title

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:14.960
is going to show up when
we go to create an API key

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.930
and when we go to restrict
that key to only be

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:20.890
able to call certain services.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.750
Once we have host
and title configured,

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.050
the next thing to do is to
add in routing instructions

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.740
for API gateway.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.410
So for each of these
resources, we're

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:30.970
going to use the
x-google-backend

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:31.840
that you see here.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:33.548
And we're going to
put in an address that

00:11:33.548 --> 00:11:35.315
corresponds to the
correct service that's

00:11:35.315 --> 00:11:36.190
running in Cloud Run.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:38.890
So for example, for
the customers resource,

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:41.180
we want to write to
the customer service.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:42.530
So how do we do that?

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:44.170
First thing that
we have to know is

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:47.620
the address for each of
our services on Cloud Run.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:50.590
And so to find the address
that we want to route to,

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.900
we can navigate back
to Cloud console

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.700
and find the service
that we're interested in.

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.400
And if we click on
the service, it's

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.420
going to bring us to
a more detailed view.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:01.150
And you can see
that, right up here,

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.670
we have the address where
the service is accessible.

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.960
And we can copy this
to our clipboard.

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:11.410
And then we will
jump back to the IDE

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.410
and back to our open
API specification.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.570
And then here on
the address field,

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.260
we'll just simply paste this in.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:21.730
So here is our address
for the customer service

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:23.800
that's currently
running on Cloud Run.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:26.740
Next we'll do that exact same
thing for the account API.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.160
We will grab its
URL from Cloud Run.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:32.950
We'll come back to the IDE and
paste that value in the address

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:35.500
field here.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:38.260
Then we'll go back to
the cloud console again,

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.110
this time grabbing
our orders API,

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:44.387
copy the location
for this service.

00:12:44.387 --> 00:12:45.970
And just like before,
we'll paste that

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:48.280
into the address field here.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:52.150
At this point, our open API
specification is ready to go.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:57.062
And we can use this to create a
new API inside of API gateway.

00:12:57.062 --> 00:12:59.020
To do that, we're going
to go to Cloud console.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.500
We're going to navigate down
to the API gateway option.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.850
And we're going to
click on Create API.

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:08.380
Here we're going to give
our API a display name.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:12.360
We'll call it ecommerce.API.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.540
And we need to use
the same API ID here

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:18.340
that we used in our
open API specification.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.130
So we called it a
ecommerce there.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.210
We need to make sure we call
it ecommerce here as well.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.210
Now, this step takes a
little while to complete.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:29.310
It's usually one to two minutes
or so for it to get done.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.810
And then once it
does wrap up, we're

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.915
ready then to upload our open
API specification that we just

00:13:33.915 --> 00:13:34.415
created.

00:13:34.415 --> 00:13:36.120
So we'll click on Browse.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:39.370
We'll find the file
in our file system.

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:45.060
And once we do, we'll
select that, open API file.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.910
And then we'll give
our configuration here

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:49.050
a display name.

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:52.050
And this will just show up on
the list when we go to the UI.

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:54.480
And then we're going to
select the service account

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.920
that we want to use to
connect to that backend,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.350
to the Cloud Run services
that we are routing to.

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.000
So again, this step
is going to take

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.760
you know around 30
seconds or so to complete.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.890
And then once this
wraps up, we'll

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:12.540
be able to deploy our new
API config to an API gateway.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.830
So just like before, we're
going to give our API

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:19.370
gateway a display name--

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:22.630
we'll call it the
commerce API gateway--

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:24.040
and pick a location.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:24.917
We'll use US Central.

00:14:24.917 --> 00:14:27.250
That's where all of our Cloud
Run services are deployed.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:28.960
Click Create.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:29.950
And there we go.

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:31.270
It's created.

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:34.150
I can now click on this API.

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:36.820
And if I do, I'll
see that I have

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:38.110
a gateway associated with it.

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:38.990
This is deployed.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.620
And this gateway is
accepting traffic at the URL

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:44.830
that you see listed
under gateway URL here.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.110
Now, I can take this URL.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:48.530
I can go to my browser.

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:50.230
And I can just simply
issue a request.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:52.900
And I'll call the gateway
URL slash customers.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:54.650
And voila, there we go.

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:57.430
We get our data back
from our customers API.

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:59.020
So now let's jump
back into Slides.

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:02.340
And let's recap what
we saw in this demo.

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:04.170
All right, so what do
we see in that demo?

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:07.530
Well, we started off with
our open API specification.

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.660
And we started off by
defining all the resources

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.460
that our API is going to
expose to the clients.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:15.750
And then we configured API
gateway with the parameters

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:16.800
that API gateway needs.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:21.690
And that was really our API ID,
our title, in our project ID.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:24.150
And then finally, we updated
each of those resources

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.670
to point to the correct
service on Cloud Run

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.600
so that when an
API call comes in,

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:31.740
it's going to be routed
to the right place.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:36.260
Once we had our open API
spec all ready to go,

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:38.270
we went back into Pantheon.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:40.497
And we configured API gateway.

00:15:40.497 --> 00:15:43.080
Now, you saw that there were a
few steps that we went through.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:46.040
And one of those steps is
we uploaded our open API

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:47.360
specification.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.490
And we created an API config.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:51.530
And in the demo, we
just created one.

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:53.570
And we took that one
API configuration.

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.570
And we deployed it directly
to the API gateway.

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:59.420
But APIs can actually have
multiple configurations

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:00.700
associated with them.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.080
And this can be pretty useful
for a number of use cases.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.600
If you're doing something
like API versioning

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.670
or you're building out
an SLDC, maybe you're

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:10.100
going to have a different
configuration in your test,

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:12.410
versus dev, versus
prod environment,

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:16.610
API configs can help you
to solve those use cases.

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:18.830
And right now,
after our demo, this

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:20.400
is what our
environment looks like.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:23.390
We have three different services
that are running on Cloud Run.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.780
We have an API gateway now
with an API called ecommerce

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:28.980
that's fronting those services.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.250
And then our clients now
have a consistent interface

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.800
that they can call
into to access

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:37.460
any pieces of those services
that are running in Cloud Run.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:38.780
Now, there is just one problem.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:40.680
That API is wide
open to the internet.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:44.060
So anyone at this point
can make a call to our API.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.860
And it's going to
respond with data.

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:50.300
So what we'd like to do is we'd
like to put in place security

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.150
so that not just anybody can
call into our API, but only

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:54.950
clients that we authorize.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:57.080
And so why don't we
jump back into the demo

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:01.400
and look at how we can use API
gateway to add API key security

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:06.160
to provide a more secure
API for our clients.

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:08.710
To configure API security,
we're going to come back

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:10.750
to our open API specification.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:13.000
As you can see, I have a
new security definition

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:14.369
that I've already added.

00:17:14.369 --> 00:17:16.540
It has a type of API key.

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:17.950
It has a name of key.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:20.270
And it has an n value query.

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:23.990
So what this means is that I'm
going to look for an API key.

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:26.560
I'm going to look for a
query parameter called key.

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:30.220
And that's what I'm going
to interpret as the API key.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:34.000
Now, I need to then go to
each individual resource.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.060
And I need to add in
a security definition.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:38.650
And I'm going to
just simply reference

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:42.270
that API key that I had in
the security definition above.

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.020
And what this is
going to do is it's

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:48.340
going to then require any
incoming API calls to either

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:51.160
provide a valid API key
that's been authorized

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:53.170
to use this resource.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:56.260
Or else it's going
to reject the call.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.020
Now that we've configured
security within our open API

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:02.770
specification, we can come
back here to the Cloud Console.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:06.220
And we can find the same
ecommerce API and the same API

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:08.590
gateway that we created
in the previous demo.

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:11.510
And we need to create a
new API configuration.

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:14.980
So to do that, let's go to the
Config tab that you see here.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:17.613
And we're going to
click on Upload Config.

00:18:17.613 --> 00:18:19.030
Just like we did
in the last demo,

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:20.322
we're going to click on Browse.

00:18:20.322 --> 00:18:23.110
We're going to find that open
API specification that we just

00:18:23.110 --> 00:18:25.690
created with our security
definitions in it.

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:28.190
We're going to select
that file and upload it.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:31.010
Now we'll give it a new
display name here as well.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:34.395
We'll call this one
ecommerce secure config.

00:18:34.395 --> 00:18:35.770
And then, just
like before, we're

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:38.500
going to select the
service account that we're

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:42.490
going to use to authenticate
the calls to Cloud Run.

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:43.868
We'll pick the same gateway.

00:18:43.868 --> 00:18:45.910
We could have created a
new gateway here as well,

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:48.730
but we're going to just override
the config that was deployed

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:50.860
to that existing gateway.

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:53.950
And we will wait for
around 30 seconds

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:57.730
or so while our configuration
is uploaded and deployed

00:18:57.730 --> 00:18:59.880
to that new gateway.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.150
And after patiently waiting
for a few seconds here,

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:07.260
we will see that this step
will complete successfully.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.990
As soon as this step wraps
up, our configuration

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.960
is live and is deployed
to our API gateway.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:14.670
So just like we did
in the last demo,

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:17.130
we can come back over here
to our browser window.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:20.130
And we can issue a request
to our gateway URL slash

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:20.910
customers.

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.160
But this time we're going
to get a different response.

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:24.720
Last time we got back data.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.280
We got back customer data.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:28.930
This time we're getting back
an error message saying,

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:31.380
hey, this API is expecting
you to provide an API key,

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:33.030
but you didn't provide one.

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:34.680
So now let's take
a look at what we

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.375
need to do to get
valid credential

00:19:36.375 --> 00:19:39.630
so that we can call this API.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:42.810
If you recall when we were very
first setting up our open API

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:46.050
specification, I said, we're
going to use this API ID.

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:49.360
And we're going to put that
ID into the title field.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.870
So let me show you
exactly how that title

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:56.520
value is going to be
important to API consumers.

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:59.350
Before we can get a valid
key to call our API,

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:03.128
we need to enable this API
within our GCP project.

00:20:03.128 --> 00:20:04.920
And the way we're going
to do that is we're

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.620
going to come here to the
APIs and Services dashboard,

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.490
click on Enable
APIs and Services.

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:12.435
And that title,
that ecommerce title

00:20:12.435 --> 00:20:14.340
is what we're going
to search for.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.590
And that's the API that we're
going to find right here.

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:20.940
And once this pops up,
we can click on Enable.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:22.650
It's going to take
a few seconds.

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.720
It's going to say this
is not a Google API

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.730
and give you a
little warning there.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:29.460
But after a few seconds,
it's going to then

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.110
be enabled on our project.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:35.640
And once it is, we
can then navigate over

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:41.710
to the APIs and Services
Credential section.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.530
And we can create
a new credential.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:45.915
In this case, it's
going to be an API key.

00:20:45.915 --> 00:20:47.520
That key is created.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.650
And then we're going to
want to restrict that key.

00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:52.840
Otherwise, it's going to
give us access to everything.

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:54.855
Once we click on
restrict key, we're

00:20:54.855 --> 00:20:56.980
going to see a list of
services we can select from.

00:20:56.980 --> 00:21:00.490
Now, because we enabled
the ecommerce API,

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.450
we're going to see
ecommerce in that list.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.420
And now this key
that we're generating

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:07.090
is only going to be
good for making calls

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:09.880
to our ecommerce API.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:11.800
Now we can click here.

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:13.805
And we can copy our
key to a clipboard.

00:21:13.805 --> 00:21:15.430
And then we'll jump
back to the browser

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:16.870
where we had the error before.

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:18.400
And let's put in
a query parameter

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.860
called key equals API key.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.400
And there we go.

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:24.470
Now we're getting back
data instead of the error.

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:25.740
Our API is secured.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.490
We have a way to get
credentials and a way

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.620
to get access to our API.

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:31.180
So now let's jump
back into slides.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:34.690
And let's recap what we did
and what we saw in this demo.

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:37.360
All right, so great, we've
set up our API gateway.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:39.880
We have configured it to point
to our Cloud Run services.

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:42.520
And now we've added
security on top of it.

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:44.080
So we're done, right?

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:46.810
Well, in a sense, yes-- but
if this were a real production

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:49.120
API, as you know,
we're going to have

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.443
to actually operate,
and maintain,

00:21:51.443 --> 00:21:52.360
and manage this thing.

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:54.693
And a big part of that is
going to be monitoring our API

00:21:54.693 --> 00:21:57.130
to make sure that it's
working the way we expect.

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:59.230
So why don't we jump
into our next demo

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:02.083
now and take a look at how
we can use cloud logging

00:22:02.083 --> 00:22:03.750
and cloud monitoring
to set up an alert,

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:06.040
so that if something is
going wrong with our API,

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:08.685
we'll get alerted about it.

00:22:08.685 --> 00:22:10.560
For our last demo, we're
going to take a look

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:11.907
at how we might monitor APIs.

00:22:11.907 --> 00:22:13.740
So to do that, we're
going to come down here

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:17.670
to the operation section of
the Cloud Console menu, pick

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:22.240
Monitoring, and then Alerting
underneath the Monitoring menu.

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:24.730
To start off, we're going
to create a new policy.

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:27.160
And let's talk a little bit
about what types of things

00:22:27.160 --> 00:22:28.580
we might want to monitor.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.580
We might want to look at
things like error rates

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:32.060
that are creeping up too high.

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:34.900
We might want to look at
the amount of traffic that's

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:36.282
coming through our API.

00:22:36.282 --> 00:22:37.990
Or in this case, what
we're going to show

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:41.830
is how we can monitor the
backend latency and get

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:44.650
alerted if all of a sudden
our backend for our API

00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:49.950
is taking longer to respond
than a specific threshold.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:52.650
Let's start by giving our
alerting policy a name.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.740
We're going to call
it slow backend.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:56.435
And then we're going
to add a condition.

00:22:56.435 --> 00:22:59.930
The first thing we need to do
is define the right resource

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.350
that we want to monitor.

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:04.070
Now, for API gateway, all
of the resources and metrics

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:05.570
that we're going
to report are going

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:09.382
to be underneath the
produced API resource.

00:23:09.382 --> 00:23:11.090
So that's what we're
going to select here

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:13.160
for our first condition.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:14.630
Once we've selected
that resource,

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:16.670
we can pick from a
number of metrics.

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:20.330
In this case, we're going to
pick request backend latency.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:23.240
From here, we need to
select the aggregator.

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:26.330
We could look at things here
like the 50th percentile,

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:27.380
95th percentile.

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:30.380
In this case, I'm going to pick
the 99th percentile, because I

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:33.170
want to understand what the
worst latency the consumers

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:36.210
are experiencing.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:37.650
And then the last
thing that we're

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:39.510
going to need to do
under Configuration

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:41.157
is to set a threshold.

00:23:41.157 --> 00:23:42.990
I'm going to set that
pretty low, just to 10

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:45.600
milliseconds, just to make
sure it's going to be easy

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:47.100
for us to trigger
this alert and we

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:50.358
can verify that it's working.

00:23:50.358 --> 00:23:51.900
And then the very
last thing we'll do

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.250
is we'll just click on Add.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:56.550
And then that will bring
us back to our main

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.617
create alerting
policy form here.

00:23:58.617 --> 00:24:00.450
And the next thing we're
going to need to do

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:03.060
is to set up a
notification channel.

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:05.310
We have different types
that we can choose from.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:08.070
I'm just going to do a
very simple notification

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:09.390
channel here of email.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:12.390
And I'm going to enter my
own email address in here.

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:16.370
We'll click on Add.

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.830
And then that's going to be it.

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:20.940
We can add documentation
if we want.

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:22.730
But I'm just going
to click on Save.

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:25.940
And now at this point,
our alert is created.

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.240
And our next step is going
to be to start generating

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:31.730
some traffic to see if
we can trigger this alert

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:35.780
and make sure that we're getting
an email if our latencies start

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:37.110
to creep up.

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:39.140
So all we have to do to
generate load-- let's

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:41.572
just come back over to the
browser we were using before.

00:24:41.572 --> 00:24:44.030
And we're just going to start
hitting our backend with just

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:45.800
a whole bunch of API calls.

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:48.830
We'll just keep smashing
the Refresh button

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:50.750
and sending those API calls.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:53.570
And hopefully that will be
enough to trigger an alert here

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:55.520
before too long.

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:58.140
If we go back to the
monitoring UI now and refresh,

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.200
we should see a new
alert that's popped up.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:01.220
And we do.

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:03.650
If we click on this, we'll
be able to get some details

00:25:03.650 --> 00:25:06.240
about the alert,
exactly what happened,

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.050
what was the latency
that we were seeing,

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:11.810
verify that it did
exceed the threshold.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:13.790
And then if I go
to my inbox, I'll

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:17.540
also see that I did get
notified about this alert being

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:18.380
triggered.

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:21.440
And now if I were the person
responsible for monitoring

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:24.538
this API and operating this
API, I could go dig into this

00:25:24.538 --> 00:25:25.830
and figure out what's going on.

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:27.205
And we can take
corrective action

00:25:27.205 --> 00:25:30.920
to make sure that we correct
any problems that are occurring.

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:32.420
Hopefully this gives
you a good idea

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:34.337
for how you can set up
monitoring and alerting

00:25:34.337 --> 00:25:36.913
for APIs that are managed
by the API gateway.

00:25:36.913 --> 00:25:39.080
And with that, let's jump
back into slides and recap

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:41.650
what we saw here in this demo.

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:44.440
All right, so now we've
seen how to build APIs.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:46.120
We've seen how to
secure those APIs.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:48.310
And now we've seen how
to monitor those APIs

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:50.810
and make sure that we're going
to get an alert if anything's

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:51.860
not working correctly.

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:54.520
So we hope that through this
demo and the presentation,

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.160
you've gotten a taste for what
you can do with our new API

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:59.200
gateway product, which,
again, we just recently

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:00.790
announced in beta.

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:02.715
I wanted to spend a
little bit of time talking

00:26:02.715 --> 00:26:04.840
to you about the things
that we have on our roadmap

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:07.540
and where we see this
product evolving over

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:09.380
the coming months.

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:11.620
So the first thing
to talk about is

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:14.710
API gateway and how we see
this serving GCP customers.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:16.535
If you're someone who
is building web apps,

00:26:16.535 --> 00:26:18.910
you're building mobile apps
or multi-experience apps that

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:21.790
are using APIs on
GCP, we really want

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:24.340
to make that process
a lot easier for you,

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:26.380
not only just routing to
things like serverless,

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:28.340
but also helping
you compose APIs.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.040
So if you have data
that's in BigQuery,

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:32.380
you have data that is
running on serverless

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:35.693
or non-serverless
compute, maybe even

00:26:35.693 --> 00:26:37.360
data that's in your
data center that you

00:26:37.360 --> 00:26:39.940
want to connect to
via VPC, we want

00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:42.910
to make that process
extremely easy for you.

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:45.328
If you're building out apps
that need authentication

00:26:45.328 --> 00:26:47.620
and you want to use things
like Google Cloud's Identity

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:49.840
Platform, we're working
closely with those teams

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:51.520
to bring you a
solution that's going

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:55.480
to make it much, much easier to
build those applications on top

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:57.700
of Google Cloud.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:59.530
Now, specifically
in our short term,

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:02.920
we have a few enhancements that
I'd like to make you aware of.

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:07.270
We've had an overwhelming
amount of demand for open API V3

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:07.790
support.

00:27:07.790 --> 00:27:09.520
And we've heard
you loud and clear.

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.070
Our team is working
hard to implement open

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:14.815
API V3 for API gateway.

00:27:14.815 --> 00:27:16.690
We're expecting to have
something out for you

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:20.033
in the very, very
first part of 2021.

00:27:20.033 --> 00:27:22.450
We're also working to integrate
with the Google Cloud Load

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:22.980
Balancer.

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:24.730
And that's going to
do a couple of things.

00:27:24.730 --> 00:27:26.410
That's going to
give you the ability

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.770
to configure integration with
products such as Cloud Armor.

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:32.170
So if you need DDoS protection,
you need [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:35.650
top 10 protection, IP
filtering, and so forth, it's

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.420
going to be a great
solution for you,

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:41.860
as well as integration with
our cloud CDN for caching

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:43.892
those responses.

00:27:43.892 --> 00:27:46.350
For those of you that are more
interested in full lifecycle

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:48.030
API management,
we're working really

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:50.190
closely with the
Apigee team as well

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:53.040
to bring you integration with
Apigee features such as API

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.460
products and Apigee's
developer portals.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:00.030
So on behalf of the entire
team here at GCP, especially

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:04.860
the serverless team, the Apigee
team, and the API gateway team,

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:07.140
we are so thankful for
you taking the time

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:08.880
to watch our session here today.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.280
And we really look forward
to getting your feedback

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:12.660
on the product.

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:14.660
Thank you so much.

